# STATION 5: notify, title, sound, anim.
npc: @test_feedback
title: Station 5 - Feedback
speaker: Tester

== start
TEST 5: Feedback. Each option should produce something visible or audible outside this window.
-> Toast notification (expect a small notification in the corner: "LowTalk test" / "Notification works")
    <<notify "LowTalk test" "Notification works" success>>
    <<jump start>>
-> Warning toast (expect an orange/warning style)
    <<notify "Careful" "This is the warning style" warning>>
    <<jump start>>
-> Titles, one per style
    <<jump titles>>
-> Play a sound at the NPC (expect the item-pickup sound)
    <<sound SFX_Player_Pickup_Item>>
    <<jump start>>
-> NPC animation (expect the NPC to wave or emote; if nothing happens, note it)
    <<anim Wave>>
    <<jump start>>
-> Leave
    <<end>>

== titles
Titles. The styles are the game's own, so a server has the ones its version shipped: Default and Major on every
version, GoblinBreach and VoidEviction from 0.7. Each should look plainly different from the others.
-> Default (no style named; expect a small title for 3 seconds)
    <<title "Default title" "Secondary line">>
    <<jump titles>>
-> Default, written the old way (expect exactly the same as above: "minor" still means Default)
    <<title "Written as minor" "Same as the default" minor>>
    <<jump titles>>
-> Major (expect a big cinematic title for 4 seconds)
    <<title "MAJOR TITLE" "The grove remembers" Major 4>>
    <<jump titles>>
-> Goblin Breach (0.7 only; expect the game's goblin-breach treatment)
    <<title "GOBLIN BREACH" "they come through the pass" GoblinBreach 4>>
    <<jump titles>>
-> Void Eviction (0.7 only; expect the game's void-eviction treatment)
    <<title "VOID EVICTION" "the dark lets go" VoidEviction 4>>
    <<jump titles>>
-> Back
    <<jump start>>

# On a server older than 0.7 the last two options close this window with "there was a problem with this
# conversation", and the log says which style it could not find. That is the refusal working, not a fault in the
# station: a style the server does not have is named rather than approximated, because approximating it would
# show the wrong title and say nothing about why.
